        {
          "sectionId": "sec-trampa-cuando-no-mejorar-algoritmo",
          "slug": "trampa-cuando-no-mejorar-algoritmo",
          "title": "10.5 Typical trap: when NOT to improve an algorithm",
          "order": 6,
          "kind": "troubleshooting",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 3,
          "learningObjectives": [
            {
              "objectiveId": "evitar-mejora-falsa",
              "text": "Avoid calling a reduction of constants an algorithmic improvement when the order of growth does not change."
            }
          ],
          "prerequisites": {
            "modules": [
              {
                "id": "mod-loop-invariant",
                "kind": "required"
              },
              {
                "id": "mod-algoritmos-iterativos-patrones-costos",
                "kind": "required"
              }
            ],
            "sections": []
          },
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-trampa-no-mejorar-parrafo-1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "When the analysis shows that the growth order is the same in the best and worst case, such as Bubble Sort with"
                },
                {
                  "type": "inlineMath",
                  "latex": "O(n^2)"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In both, there is no way to improve the behavior of the algorithm without fundamentally changing its design."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-trampa-no-mejorar-parrafo-2",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "If you are asked to “improve” the efficiency function but cannot lower the order, you can try to reduce the constants, but this only helps to"
                },
                {
                  "type": "inlineMath",
                  "latex": "n"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "small. For"
                },
                {
                  "type": "inlineMath",
                  "latex": "n"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "large, asymptotic behavior dominates."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-trampa-no-mejorar-callout",
              "type": "note",
              "variant": "warning",
              "title": "Care",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "blk-trampa-no-mejorar-callout-text",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Reducing constants may be useful in implementation, but it is not an asymptotic improvement. If the order remains the same, the growth family did not change."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
